1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:08,778
Somewhere in a contract, a 512-token chunk
boundary just landed between

2
00:00:08,778 --> 00:00:12,975
"Notwithstanding the foregoing" and
the rule it modifies.

3
00:00:13,583 --> 00:00:18,859
Your retrieval system will now find the rule
without its exception, and answer with

4
00:00:18,859 --> 00:00:19,558
confidence.

5
00:00:19,858 --> 00:00:24,408
In this video, we'll look at how to chunk
contracts so that doesn't happen.

6
00:00:25,417 --> 00:00:27,217
Here's the core idea.

7
00:00:27,517 --> 00:00:31,567
Contracts are highly structured, and
full of cross-references.

8
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:36,367
So chunk them along their own
hierarchy: article, section, clause.

9
00:00:36,983 --> 00:00:42,469
Then give each chunk the context it depends on:
where it sits, whose contract it is,

10
00:00:42,469 --> 00:00:44,558
and what its defined terms mean.

11
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:48,933
First, fixed windows split
clauses mid-sentence.

12
00:00:49,233 --> 00:00:54,125
Exceptions and carve-outs end up in a
different chunk from the rule, which is

13
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:57,683
exactly how a system produces
confidently wrong answers.

14
00:00:58,283 --> 00:01:01,058
Second, defined terms live elsewhere.

15
00:01:01,358 --> 00:01:07,683
"Confidential Information" is defined in
Section 1.4 and used in Section 9.

16
00:01:07,983 --> 00:01:11,958
A Section 9 chunk on its own
doesn't say what it covers.

17
00:01:12,583 --> 00:01:14,708
Third, cross-references.

18
00:01:15,008 --> 00:01:20,408
"Subject to Section 12.3" makes a clause
meaningless without its target.

19
00:01:21,017 --> 00:01:23,067
And fourth, boilerplate.

20
00:01:23,367 --> 00:01:28,042
A portfolio has thousands of
near-identical governing-law clauses.

21
00:01:28,342 --> 00:01:33,167
Without contract-level metadata, retrieval
can't tell whose clause it found.

22
00:01:34,183 --> 00:01:37,508
Before designing anything, pin
down four things.

23
00:01:37,808 --> 00:01:39,558
First, the task.

24
00:01:39,858 --> 00:01:45,699
Question answering over one contract is very
different from finding every contract

25
00:01:45,699 --> 00:01:47,408
with uncapped indemnity.

26
00:01:47,708 --> 00:01:52,982
Cross-corpus "find all" questions push toward
structured extraction, not chunk

27
00:01:52,982 --> 00:01:53,658
retrieval.

28
00:01:54,283 --> 00:01:56,333
Second, source quality.

29
00:01:56,633 --> 00:02:01,283
Scans and redlines decide how much you
can trust the structure parser.

30
00:02:01,883 --> 00:02:04,183
Third, size and versions.

31
00:02:04,483 --> 00:02:08,333
Contracts run from 5 to over 300 pages.

32
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:13,733
With a master agreement plus amendments, the
governing text may live in a later

33
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:17,283
document, so the system has to
know which version wins.

34
00:02:17,883 --> 00:02:19,783
And fourth, precision.

35
00:02:20,083 --> 00:02:25,458
Legal users need the exact clause text, with
a citation to the section number.

36
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:28,808
Roughly relevant passages
aren't good enough.

37
00:02:29,817 --> 00:02:32,342
Here's the design in one picture.

38
00:02:32,642 --> 00:02:37,718
The chunk that gets retrieved is small, but the
context the model sees is assembled

39
00:02:37,718 --> 00:02:39,492
from the structure around it.

40
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:42,392
It starts with a structure tree.

41
00:02:42,692 --> 00:02:49,367
Detect the numbering hierarchy, from Article 9,
to Section 9.2, to clause 9.2(b).

42
00:02:49,667 --> 00:02:55,892
Use layout cues and regular expressions, with
an LLM fallback for messy documents.

43
00:02:56,517 --> 00:03:01,517
Keep tables like payment schedules and
SLAs intact, as Markdown.

44
00:03:01,817 --> 00:03:05,417
And treat exhibits and schedules
as separate subtrees.

45
00:03:06,417 --> 00:03:11,733
Then make one chunk per clause: the lowest
heading that fits within about 800

46
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:12,217
tokens.

47
00:03:12,817 --> 00:03:19,092
Merge tiny siblings, like one-line sub-clauses,
up to a minimum of about 200 tokens.

48
00:03:19,717 --> 00:03:25,706
And split oversized clauses at their sub-clause
boundaries, with a small overlap of

49
00:03:25,706 --> 00:03:26,717
1-2 sentences.

50
00:03:27,717 --> 00:03:30,667
The chunk count follows from
contract length.

51
00:03:30,967 --> 00:03:35,117
A 40-page contract is roughly 20k tokens.

52
00:03:35,717 --> 00:03:41,342
With clause chunks averaging about 300
tokens, that's about 65 chunks.

53
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:49,875
A 300-page master agreement with schedules,
about 150k tokens, produces about 500.

54
00:03:50,175 --> 00:03:53,775
So expect tens to a few hundred
chunks per contract.

55
00:03:54,783 --> 00:04:01,333
Next, prepend a context header to every chunk,
for embedding and BM25 indexing.

56
00:04:01,633 --> 00:04:06,777
It names the contract, its parties and effective
date, and the heading path down to

57
00:04:06,777 --> 00:04:07,458
the clause.

58
00:04:07,758 --> 00:04:10,008
Store it separately for display.

59
00:04:10,617 --> 00:04:16,583
Optionally, add a 1-2 sentence summary,
generated by an LLM, that situates the

60
00:04:16,583 --> 00:04:18,342
clause in its contract.

61
00:04:18,642 --> 00:04:21,692
Anthropic calls this contextual retrieval.

62
00:04:21,992 --> 00:04:27,571
Contract portfolios are full of near-identical
boilerplate, so it matters more for

63
00:04:27,571 --> 00:04:29,817
legal text than for most domains.

64
00:04:30,817 --> 00:04:33,942
The cost is modest, but check
the assumptions.

65
00:04:34,242 --> 00:04:41,572
Anthropic reported $1.02 per million document
tokens, with a small model and prompt

66
00:04:41,572 --> 00:04:43,692
caching, at 2024 prices.

67
00:04:44,317 --> 00:04:49,382
Each chunk's call re-reads the cached
document, so the cost grows with document

68
00:04:49,382 --> 00:04:51,242
length divided by chunk size.

69
00:04:51,542 --> 00:04:58,439
Redo the math for 30k-token contracts and
300-token chunks, and it's about $4 per

70
00:04:58,439 --> 00:04:59,717
million tokens.

71
00:05:00,317 --> 00:05:08,782
So 10,000 such contracts, 300M tokens, cost on
the order of $1,200 to contextualize

72
00:05:08,782 --> 00:05:09,292
once.

73
00:05:09,592 --> 00:05:12,542
Re-price with the model you actually use.

74
00:05:13,550 --> 00:05:15,550
Now the retrieval side.

75
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:19,650
Extract the definitions section
into a lookup table.

76
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:25,717
At retrieval time, attach the definitions of
the capitalized terms each retrieved

77
00:05:25,717 --> 00:05:26,500
chunk uses.

78
00:05:27,117 --> 00:05:31,367
Don't inline definitions into
every chunk at index time.

79
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:34,642
It bloats the chunk, and
dilutes its embedding.

80
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:37,775
Next, build a cross-reference graph.

81
00:05:38,075 --> 00:05:43,222
At generation time, pull in one hop of
referenced clauses, at least for "subject to"

82
00:05:43,222 --> 00:05:48,369
and "notwithstanding" links, since those change
the meaning of the clause that cites

83
00:05:48,369 --> 00:05:48,675
them.

84
00:05:49,283 --> 00:05:51,708
And retrieve small, then expand.

85
00:05:52,008 --> 00:05:57,155
Retrieve on clauses, and expand to the parent
section when the answer needs the

86
00:05:57,155 --> 00:05:58,458
surrounding context.

87
00:05:59,083 --> 00:06:04,082
Keep one contract-level summary chunk too,
for routing questions like "which

88
00:06:04,082 --> 00:06:05,858
contracts are with Globex?"

89
00:06:06,883 --> 00:06:12,354
Store metadata for filtering: contract type,
parties, dates, governing law, and

90
00:06:12,354 --> 00:06:12,908
version.

91
00:06:13,517 --> 00:06:18,860
And record amendment lineage, with a
superseded-by link, so retrieval prefers the

92
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:20,642
terms that currently apply.

93
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:25,600
There's also an alternative to context
headers, called late chunking.

94
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:30,921
A long-context embedding model reads the whole
document first, then pools token

95
00:06:30,921 --> 00:06:35,625
embeddings per chunk, so each chunk vector
already knows its surroundings.

96
00:06:35,925 --> 00:06:38,675
It needs no LLM call per chunk.

97
00:06:38,975 --> 00:06:43,625
Include it in your comparison, rather than
assuming either approach wins.

98
00:06:44,650 --> 00:06:50,166
Legal queries mix meaning, like "cap on
liability", with exact strings, like

99
00:06:50,166 --> 00:06:54,375
"Section 7.3", LIBOR in legacy
contracts, and party names.

100
00:06:54,675 --> 00:06:59,175
So use hybrid search: BM25
plus dense retrieval.

101
00:06:59,783 --> 00:07:01,983
Be careful with the reranker.

102
00:07:02,283 --> 00:07:07,946
In the LegalBench-RAG experiments, adding a
general-purpose reranker performed worse

103
00:07:07,946 --> 00:07:13,002
than no reranker at all on legal text, which
the authors put down to domain

104
00:07:13,002 --> 00:07:13,608
mismatch.

105
00:07:13,908 --> 00:07:18,833
Evaluate any off-the-shelf reranker on
legal queries before you adopt it.

106
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:24,036
And generation should quote the clause
verbatim, with its section number, so a

107
00:07:24,036 --> 00:07:26,800
lawyer can check the answer
against the source.

108
00:07:27,817 --> 00:07:32,167
Chunking is a hyperparameter you
measure, not one you argue about.

109
00:07:32,467 --> 00:07:37,717
So build an eval of 200-500 queries
with gold clause spans.

110
00:07:38,317 --> 00:07:40,742
Two public resources help.

111
00:07:41,042 --> 00:07:48,613
CUAD has 510 commercial contracts, with more
than 13,000 expert labels across 41

112
00:07:48,613 --> 00:07:50,317
clause categories.

113
00:07:50,917 --> 00:07:57,612
LegalBench-RAG has 6,858 query-answer pairs,
with ground truth given as character

114
00:07:57,612 --> 00:08:00,092
spans in the source documents.

115
00:08:00,392 --> 00:08:04,042
Then add in-house questions that
reflect your users.

116
00:08:05,050 --> 00:08:06,925
Measure three things.

117
00:08:07,225 --> 00:08:09,400
Span-level recall at k.

118
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:12,875
Did any retrieved chunk
overlap the gold span?

119
00:08:13,483 --> 00:08:15,283
Precision at k.

120
00:08:15,583 --> 00:08:19,108
How much irrelevant text did the
model have to read?

121
00:08:19,408 --> 00:08:25,058
A 2,000-token chunk that contains a
40-token answer is mostly noise.

122
00:08:25,683 --> 00:08:29,733
And answer correctness, on a subset
labeled by attorneys.

123
00:08:30,750 --> 00:08:35,300
Then hold the rest of the pipeline fixed,
and compare chunkers head to head.

124
00:08:35,917 --> 00:08:40,367
Fixed 512-token windows, with 10% overlap.

125
00:08:40,983 --> 00:08:43,308
Recursive character splitting.

126
00:08:43,917 --> 00:08:46,017
Structure-aware clause chunks.

127
00:08:46,617 --> 00:08:49,267
Clause chunks with context headers.

128
00:08:49,883 --> 00:08:51,658
And late chunking.

129
00:08:51,958 --> 00:08:57,239
Clause chunks with context should beat fixed
windows, but the published evidence is

130
00:08:57,239 --> 00:09:00,483
thin, so produce the numbers on
your own contracts.

131
00:09:00,783 --> 00:09:06,083
Slice them by contract length, scanned versus
native documents, and query type.

132
00:09:06,383 --> 00:09:09,883
Averages hide the slice where a
chunker collapses.

133
00:09:10,883 --> 00:09:12,683
Here are the tradeoffs.

134
00:09:12,983 --> 00:09:17,133
Fixed-size windows are trivial, and
robust to bad parsing.

135
00:09:17,433 --> 00:09:22,633
But they split clauses, so use them only as a
baseline, or for scans you can't

136
00:09:22,633 --> 00:09:23,033
parse.

137
00:09:23,650 --> 00:09:28,450
Clause chunks with context headers line up
with how lawyers cite and query.

138
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:33,250
They need a good parser, and an LLM
call per chunk at ingest.

139
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:36,125
This is the default for contracts.

140
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:41,325
Putting the whole contract in a long
context has no chunking errors.

141
00:09:41,625 --> 00:09:46,650
But it's expensive per query, and recall
drops in the middle of long contexts.

142
00:09:46,950 --> 00:09:51,350
It fits single-contract questions,
under about 100 pages.

143
00:09:51,950 --> 00:09:57,306
And structured extraction into a table is
exact and auditable, but needs a fixed

144
00:09:57,306 --> 00:09:57,775
schema.

145
00:09:58,075 --> 00:10:00,900
That's the tool for portfolio analytics.

146
00:10:01,917 --> 00:10:03,792
Which matters at scale.

147
00:10:04,092 --> 00:10:09,715
A question like "all contracts with
auto-renewal and under 30 days' notice" is the

148
00:10:09,715 --> 00:10:11,292
wrong job for chunking.

149
00:10:11,917 --> 00:10:17,067
Extract clauses into a structured table at
ingest, and query it with SQL.

150
00:10:17,367 --> 00:10:20,067
RAG then handles only the long tail.

151
00:10:21,083 --> 00:10:23,408
Finally, the common mistakes.

152
00:10:23,708 --> 00:10:29,715
Choosing chunk size by intuition, because 512
feels right, instead of measuring

153
00:10:29,715 --> 00:10:31,083
span-level recall.

154
00:10:31,683 --> 00:10:35,933
Separating exceptions and carve-outs
from the rule they modify.

155
00:10:36,233 --> 00:10:40,508
The answers come out confidently wrong,
in the costliest direction.

156
00:10:41,117 --> 00:10:46,233
Leaving contract identity out of the chunk, so
a near-identical clause from the

157
00:10:46,233 --> 00:10:47,917
wrong contract gets cited.

158
00:10:48,517 --> 00:10:50,492
And ignoring amendments.

159
00:10:50,792 --> 00:10:56,624
If the system cites terms that no longer apply,
that's a liability risk, not just a

160
00:10:56,624 --> 00:10:57,467
quality bug.

161
00:10:57,767 --> 00:11:01,117
Lineage deserves an owner, and
tests of its own.

162
00:11:02,117 --> 00:11:03,792
So, to recap.

163
00:11:04,092 --> 00:11:07,692
Chunk by clause, along the
contract's own structure.

164
00:11:07,992 --> 00:11:13,370
Carry the context each clause depends on: its
header, its definitions, and its

165
00:11:13,370 --> 00:11:14,542
cross-references.

166
00:11:14,842 --> 00:11:19,217
And measure the choice on span-level
recall, on your own contracts.

167
00:11:20,217 --> 00:11:23,742
The full article, with every
source, is on vidyal.ai.

168
00:11:24,750 --> 00:11:26,525
Thanks for watching.

169
00:11:26,825 --> 00:11:33,375
Next in the series, BM25 versus dense versus
hybrid search, and when to use each.

170
00:11:33,675 --> 00:11:35,800
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
